WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:08.890
GABE WEISS: Hey, everyone.

00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:12.447
Welcome to the Google Intel
webinar on the IoT platform.

00:00:12.447 --> 00:00:14.530
We're going to start the
webinar here in a second.

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:16.790
And while it's running,
if you have any questions,

00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:18.280
just ask them into
the live chat,

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:20.380
and we'll get to
them when it's done.

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:22.180
In the meantime,
enjoy the webinar.

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:27.110
Hey, everyone.

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:28.776
Thank you for tuning
into our first show

00:00:28.776 --> 00:00:29.890
in a three-part series.

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:31.510
I'm Gabe Weiss, and
I work for Google

00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:33.469
as a developer advocate
for the Cloud Platform.

00:00:33.469 --> 00:00:34.301
MARTIN KRONBERG: Hi.

00:00:34.301 --> 00:00:36.250
And I'm Martin
Kronberg from Intel.

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:37.870
I'm a technical
evangelist working

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:40.360
with our Internet of
Things technologies

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:44.100
and integrating them with
cloud partners such as Google.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:46.600
So you know, I've been working
on creating proof of concepts

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:48.910
as well as a series of
workshops that we're

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:50.770
going to be teaching
in the near future,

00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:52.270
and I'm really
excited to share what

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:54.130
I've learned with
a broader audience

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:55.847
through these webinars.

00:00:55.847 --> 00:00:57.430
So today, we're going
to be discussing

00:00:57.430 --> 00:01:00.250
how we can use Intel and
Google technologies to create

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:03.270
a predictive
maintenance framework.

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:05.140
Predictive maintenance
is a concept

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:08.770
of predicting when a component
in a factory or an airplane

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:12.310
is going to fail and then
replacing it before it does.

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:15.160
It's based around gathering
as much data as possible

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:18.340
from the system and then using
machine learning to, at first,

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:21.580
find anomalies in the data
and then predict anomalies

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:24.160
in that data before they happen.

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:27.460
In this first webinar, we're
going to look at the basics--

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:30.220
Intel and Google's place
in the IoT ecosystem,

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:32.980
gathering the sensor data,
and ingesting the sensor data

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:35.900
into Google's Cloud Platform.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:37.840
So in order to build
out this framework,

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:41.290
we're going to be leveraging
Intel IoT hardware, Google's

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:45.490
Cloud IoT platform as well as
big data processing and machine

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:47.860
learning services
in the Google Cloud.

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:50.170
And hopefully, we can
inspire you guys to start

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:51.480
some of your own IoT projects.

00:01:56.027 --> 00:01:58.110
GABE WEISS: So before we
launch into the specifics

00:01:58.110 --> 00:01:59.651
of the framework,
let's take a minute

00:01:59.651 --> 00:02:01.980
for a higher-level
overview of both Intel

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:03.980
and Google's place
in the IoT ecosystem.

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:05.730
So Martin, why don't
you start and give us

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:09.194
a rundown of what Intel
is doing in the IoT space?

00:02:09.194 --> 00:02:10.110
MARTIN KRONBERG: Sure.

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:14.160
So, IoT starts with
endpoints, so things

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:18.060
like sensors, actuators,
the kinds of devices

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:20.580
that gather data and then
interface with the environment

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:22.320
in some way.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.870
The simplest example
might be a thermometer

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:26.910
to read temperature
and then a relay

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:29.647
to turn on an HVAC system.

00:02:29.647 --> 00:02:31.230
Now, at the other
end of the spectrum,

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:33.150
are the cloud-based analytics--

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:35.920
big data processing,
machine learning,

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:37.341
natural language processing.

00:02:37.341 --> 00:02:39.840
And this is where a lot of the
really exciting possibilities

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:42.450
are like predictive maintenance.

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:43.950
But the thing about
machine learning

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:47.370
is that, while it has this
huge potential across almost

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:51.600
all industries, it's very
compute intensive, so much so

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:54.630
that doesn't really make
sense to run it locally

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:58.050
and we have to run
it in the cloud.

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:01.680
However, once we leverage
those cloud services,

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:04.080
that thermostat can all of
a sudden do more than just

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:05.910
tell you the
temperature right now.

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:08.536
It can begin to tell you the
temperature in the future using

00:03:08.536 --> 00:03:09.410
predictive analytics.

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:11.243
GABE WEISS: And, of
course, all these things

00:03:11.243 --> 00:03:13.112
need a way to talk to
that cloud service.

00:03:13.112 --> 00:03:14.070
MARTIN KRONBERG: Right.

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:15.611
And so there's a
few issues that need

00:03:15.611 --> 00:03:18.100
to be overcome in order
to make this happen.

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:19.800
First of all, many
endpoints do not

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:23.350
have a way to connect to
the internet on their own.

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:26.640
Now, they might have a serial
connection like USB, Modbus,

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:32.139
or maybe a short-range radio
like ZigBee, Z-Wave, Bluetooth.

00:03:32.139 --> 00:03:34.680
And so this is where one of the
core aspects of the Intel IoT

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:37.740
ecosystem, or the
gateway, comes into play.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:39.990
It acts like a data
collection hub.

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:41.970
It collects all the
data from the sensors

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:45.570
and then establishes a secure
connection to the cloud.

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:48.450
Now, having a central data
collection point like this

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:51.000
allows for a few other
things to happen.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:53.940
First, even if we could connect
each one of these sensors

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:57.930
individually up to the cloud, it
would be inefficient as well as

00:03:57.930 --> 00:03:59.190
insecure.

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:01.050
The connection
points that you have

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:05.100
in an IoT system, the larger the
potential cyberattack surface

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:06.840
becomes.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:09.030
The gateway minimizes the
number of connections,

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:10.650
and it also comes
with some security

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:12.630
software that makes
that connection as

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:13.482
secure as possible.

00:04:13.482 --> 00:04:14.940
GABE WEISS: So it
makes sense, when

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:16.740
you talk about these
gateways, are you

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:18.345
thinking of a specific device?

00:04:18.345 --> 00:04:19.470
MARTIN KRONBERG: All right.

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:21.720
So there are a lot of
companies out there

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:24.030
that make these
gateway-type devices,

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:27.540
and they're going to range
in both performance, price,

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:31.207
size, as well as the kinds
of I/O that they have.

00:04:31.207 --> 00:04:32.790
However, for this
webinar, we're going

00:04:32.790 --> 00:04:35.820
to be focusing on the gateway
that comes with the Grove IoT

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:40.320
Developer Kit, and it
is an Atom-powered nook.

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:43.140
And this developer kit is
part of a larger developer

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:45.829
program at Intel.

00:04:45.829 --> 00:04:47.370
Our developer program
is really aimed

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:50.774
at getting all of the tools
that IoT developers might need

00:04:50.774 --> 00:04:52.440
as well as all the
information that they

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:55.080
need to use them in one place.

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:58.560
We have libraries, code
samples, even market studies

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:00.540
of different
verticals, as well as

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:03.600
community forums and all
kinds of training materials

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:05.467
like this very webinar.

00:05:05.467 --> 00:05:07.800
And I would recommend that
anyone interested in learning

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:12.450
more about IoT development
go to software.intel.com/IoT

00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:13.200
and check it out.

00:05:18.597 --> 00:05:20.680
GABE WEISS: So to facilitate
these devices talking

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:23.670
to the cloud, Google has created
a new Google Cloud Platform

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:25.740
product, Cloud IoT Core.

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:27.690
It's aimed at communicating
with and managing

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:28.959
these fleets of devices.

00:05:28.959 --> 00:05:31.500
It's not to say that it doesn't
work for individual tinkerers

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:34.690
as well, but the focus really is
at these large installations--

00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:36.930
planet-scale, as we like to say.

00:05:36.930 --> 00:05:39.350
Cloud IoT Core is a
fully-managed service,

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:40.980
so no need to spin anything up.

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:43.740
It just works and scales
and balances internally.

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:45.150
There's two pieces to it.

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:46.800
First is the Device
Manager, which

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:49.410
handles registering, tracking,
and configuring these devices

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:50.310
themselves.

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:53.640
Second is the communication
piece, which uses an MQTT

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:56.190
or HTTP bridge.

00:05:56.190 --> 00:05:59.499
MARTIN KRONBERG: So GCP provides
an MQTT broker to send data to?

00:05:59.499 --> 00:06:02.040
GABE WEISS: Right, although it's
not a fully-featured broker.

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:04.740
There's no configuration you can
do to the broker communication

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:05.340
part.

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:08.040
It just passes the payload
onto our messaging system,

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:08.850
Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:11.820
That's what we call a
bridge and not a broker.

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:13.290
So on the device
management side,

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:17.040
IoT Core organizes devices into
buckets we call registries.

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:20.880
Each registry maps one-to-one
to a particular Pub/Sub bucket

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:22.110
called a topic.

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:23.880
So any devices in
a given registry

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:26.400
will have all their messages
published to the associated

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:27.394
Pub/Sub topic.

00:06:27.394 --> 00:06:29.310
There's not really enough
time in this webinar

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:31.643
to go into all the things you
can do with these devices,

00:06:31.643 --> 00:06:35.070
but to touch on some of it, you
can pass configuration messages

00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:37.260
back to the devices
from IoT Core.

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:39.220
You can enable or
disable a device,

00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:42.000
assign properties to
individual devices,

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:44.970
or control the SSL key
expiration for security,

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:46.460
and more.

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:48.870
MARTIN KRONBERG: So then,
does GCP automatically

00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:50.880
refresh the SSL
certificates that

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:52.554
are needed for the connection?

00:06:52.554 --> 00:06:53.970
GABE WEISS: Not
automatically, no.

00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:56.880
So best practice is, when
the device gets created,

00:06:56.880 --> 00:06:58.860
you install a number
of certificates.

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:00.690
And then, in the
IoT Core interface,

00:07:00.690 --> 00:07:03.570
you can specify timeouts
for individual certs

00:07:03.570 --> 00:07:04.802
to do the rotation.

00:07:04.802 --> 00:07:07.260
It's easier than having to
flash new services to the device

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:08.940
after it's already
installed somewhere,

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:11.040
possibly hard to get to.

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:13.470
So on that communication
side, messages

00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:17.070
are handled simply with MQTT
being the dominant protocol.

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:20.206
Devices can send messages to
the IoT Core endpoint, which

00:07:20.206 --> 00:07:22.080
then publishes their
payload to the next link

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:23.700
in the chain, Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:25.200
We'll talk in more
detail about what

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:27.033
you can do with these
messages once they hit

00:07:27.033 --> 00:07:28.222
Pub/Sub in the next webinar.

00:07:28.222 --> 00:07:29.930
The biggest takeaway
here is that Pub/Sub

00:07:29.930 --> 00:07:31.457
subscales globally.

00:07:31.457 --> 00:07:33.540
Each topic is available
from anywhere in the world

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:35.865
and can handle over a
million messages per second.

00:07:35.865 --> 00:07:37.740
So you never have to
worry about your devices

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:38.755
overloading the system.

00:07:38.755 --> 00:07:39.630
MARTIN KRONBERG: Wow.

00:07:39.630 --> 00:07:41.700
That's a pretty
impressive bandwidth.

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:43.860
Is there a size limit to
the individual messages?

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:46.140
GABE WEISS: There is,
256k, which for devices

00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:47.850
is a lot of sensor data.

00:07:47.850 --> 00:07:49.800
But depending on the
device being used,

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:51.290
I'd never go that high.

00:07:51.290 --> 00:07:53.520
Network usage is one of
the biggest drains on power

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:55.170
for these small devices.

00:07:55.170 --> 00:07:56.640
That size is more
for the use case

00:07:56.640 --> 00:07:59.250
of a device that would
sleep for a long time,

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:01.300
gather a bunch of data,
wake up, and deliver it

00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:03.870
in the big burst, and then go
back to sleep for a long time.

00:08:09.014 --> 00:08:09.930
MARTIN KRONBERG: Cool.

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:12.430
Well, let's take a
look at this framework.

00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:14.700
So first we have a
few sensors here.

00:08:14.700 --> 00:08:16.980
So we have a light sensor.

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:19.450
We have a sound sensor.

00:08:19.450 --> 00:08:22.530
And we have a
temperature sensor.

00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:25.230
These types of sensors
are used typically

00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:27.790
in industrial
environments to monitor

00:08:27.790 --> 00:08:30.590
machinery or various processes.

00:08:30.590 --> 00:08:32.280
The sensors that we're
going to be using

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:35.409
are these Grove
prototyping sensors,

00:08:35.409 --> 00:08:38.010
which are plugged into
an Arduino 101, which

00:08:38.010 --> 00:08:40.679
we're using as a sensor hub.

00:08:40.679 --> 00:08:44.130
These sensors are really
great for quickly prototyping

00:08:44.130 --> 00:08:45.960
or creating a proof of concept.

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:50.020
They each have a socket,
which gives you power, ground,

00:08:50.020 --> 00:08:51.840
and then two signal pins.

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:54.420
So there's no need
for breadboarding.

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:57.600
These sensors in particular
came from the IoT Developer Kit

00:08:57.600 --> 00:08:59.430
which I mentioned earlier.

00:08:59.430 --> 00:09:04.212
The Arduino 101 is connected
to our nook gateway via a USB.

00:09:04.212 --> 00:09:06.420
We're going to be using the
Firmata protocol in order

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:09.930
to pass data between the
Arduino 101 and the gateway.

00:09:09.930 --> 00:09:13.350
Essentially, Firmata virtualizes
the GPIOs on the Arduino,

00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:16.210
allowing full control
from the gateway.

00:09:16.210 --> 00:09:19.440
Now, on the gateway, we're going
to be using a Python script

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:22.510
to gather the sensor data,
package that data into JSON

00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:25.230
objects, and then
connect to Google Cloud

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:26.470
to send data back and forth.

00:09:26.470 --> 00:09:27.870
GABE WEISS: So how do you
interface with the sensors

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:28.495
on the gateway?

00:09:28.495 --> 00:09:30.320
Are there libraries
that you're using?

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:31.236
MARTIN KRONBERG: Yeah.

00:09:31.236 --> 00:09:34.680
So specifically, we're going
to be using MRAA and UPM.

00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:37.950
MRAA is a low-level sensor
library created and maintained

00:09:37.950 --> 00:09:41.035
by Intel for
interfacing with GPIOs,

00:09:41.035 --> 00:09:43.980
I-squared-C buses, and SPIs.

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:46.120
Just to give you an idea
of how all this works,

00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:47.786
here's a sample of
code that we're going

00:09:47.786 --> 00:09:49.190
to be using in this project.

00:09:49.190 --> 00:09:51.690
Basically, we're using MRAA
to connect to the Arduino

00:09:51.690 --> 00:09:54.300
using the generic
Firmata protocol.

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:57.090
And then we're using UPM to
create three sensor objects,

00:09:57.090 --> 00:09:58.920
sound, light, and temperature.

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:01.020
And then using
the sensor API dot

00:10:01.020 --> 00:10:05.460
read or dot value to read
out the sensor values.

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:06.990
Pretty simple.

00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:09.360
And what's really cool
about MRAA and UPM

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:12.180
is that these libraries
are 100% open source,

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:15.660
and they're compatible with
almost all developer platforms.

00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:19.860
So you can run MRAA on
Raspberry Pi, BeagleBone,

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:21.990
all the Intel
gateway platforms--

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:24.840
UP Squared Board,
[? MidoBoard. ?] It even works

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:27.519
with some Altera FPGAs .

00:10:27.519 --> 00:10:29.060
So once again, as
a developer, you're

00:10:29.060 --> 00:10:30.929
going to have a
lot of flexibility.

00:10:30.929 --> 00:10:32.970
You can start developing
on whatever platform you

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:33.781
have access to.

00:10:33.781 --> 00:10:35.280
And then if you
decide that you need

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:37.650
to move over to
Intel architecture,

00:10:37.650 --> 00:10:39.630
you can take your code as is--

00:10:39.630 --> 00:10:40.980
for the most part--

00:10:40.980 --> 00:10:44.119
and deploy it on a
different platform.

00:10:44.119 --> 00:10:46.410
To be fair, there's going to
be some translation needed

00:10:46.410 --> 00:10:48.210
from platform to platform.

00:10:48.210 --> 00:10:49.800
For instance, not
all devices are

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:54.720
going to deploy USB to the
ttyACM0 node as a gateway has.

00:10:59.672 --> 00:11:01.130
GABE WEISS: So once
the sensor data

00:11:01.130 --> 00:11:02.660
is collected in
the gateway, then

00:11:02.660 --> 00:11:04.190
we need to get it to the cloud.

00:11:04.190 --> 00:11:05.900
This starts with the
devices implementing

00:11:05.900 --> 00:11:08.530
that and MQTT protocol
to send messages.

00:11:08.530 --> 00:11:11.690
MQTT stands for Message
Queue Telemetry Transport,

00:11:11.690 --> 00:11:14.180
and it's a very lightweight
publish/subscribe protocol

00:11:14.180 --> 00:11:16.550
that sits on top of TCP/IP.

00:11:16.550 --> 00:11:19.580
It's ideal for the IoT world
because it's so efficient.

00:11:19.580 --> 00:11:21.860
It works with the
low-power devices

00:11:21.860 --> 00:11:23.930
without draining power too fast.

00:11:23.930 --> 00:11:26.457
To talk over MQTT
to Cloud IoT Core,

00:11:26.457 --> 00:11:28.790
developers need to use a
library in the language they're

00:11:28.790 --> 00:11:29.780
using on the device.

00:11:29.780 --> 00:11:32.210
There's good ones for
Python and Node.js.

00:11:32.210 --> 00:11:35.400
There's also a C library
called Paho MQTT.

00:11:35.400 --> 00:11:37.400
MARTIN KRONBERG: So there's
no need for any kind

00:11:37.400 --> 00:11:41.454
of special GCP SDK or library?

00:11:41.454 --> 00:11:42.620
Is there one even available?

00:11:42.620 --> 00:11:44.828
GABE WEISS: Not for the
transmission of messages, no.

00:11:44.828 --> 00:11:46.430
But the whole point
was we were aiming

00:11:46.430 --> 00:11:49.080
at make it as easy as
possible for the devices.

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:52.100
So it's just this Google
endpoint for MQTT messages,

00:11:52.100 --> 00:11:52.890
and that's it.

00:11:52.890 --> 00:11:55.130
The manged service
then handles the rest.

00:11:55.130 --> 00:11:57.710
Once we send the message
and the proper protocol,

00:11:57.710 --> 00:11:59.780
that next piece is going
to be authentication.

00:11:59.780 --> 00:12:03.350
IoT Core authenticates devices
using JWT, JSON Web Tokens.

00:12:03.350 --> 00:12:06.159
I'm not going to go into
a lot of detail about it,

00:12:06.159 --> 00:12:08.450
but we can include a link to
more information about it.

00:12:08.450 --> 00:12:11.870
The tl-dr version is that it's
an encoded claim encrypted

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:15.890
with the public-private key
pair using either RSA or ECC

00:12:15.890 --> 00:12:18.380
that's transmitted
as a JSON object.

00:12:18.380 --> 00:12:19.880
Once you've got
that taken care of,

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:21.590
it's time to start
sending the data.

00:12:21.590 --> 00:12:23.540
The message payloads can
be whatever you want.

00:12:23.540 --> 00:12:26.120
Most of our examples use
JSON blobs of sensor data

00:12:26.120 --> 00:12:28.670
because it's really easy
to pull JSON directly

00:12:28.670 --> 00:12:31.554
into downstream pipelines
without too much extra work.

00:12:31.554 --> 00:12:33.470
There's a few limitations
around the messages,

00:12:33.470 --> 00:12:36.200
like that 256k size
that we mentioned.

00:12:36.200 --> 00:12:38.600
Limits are listed clearly
in the docs, though.

00:12:38.600 --> 00:12:40.400
On the cloud side,
to get all the setup,

00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:42.080
you need to have
an account on GCP

00:12:42.080 --> 00:12:43.970
with the Cloud IoT Core enabled.

00:12:43.970 --> 00:12:46.490
And from there, you can create
your device registry, which

00:12:46.490 --> 00:12:47.780
is tied to the Pub/Sub topic.

00:12:47.780 --> 00:12:49.860
And once that
registry is created,

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:53.270
then you can register all
your devices with SSL keys.

00:12:53.270 --> 00:12:55.350
And that's it to get your
basics up and running.

00:12:55.350 --> 00:12:57.320
MARTIN KRONBERG: Well, it
sounds pretty straightforward.

00:12:57.320 --> 00:12:58.694
Is there a place
where developers

00:12:58.694 --> 00:13:01.851
can go to find a step-by-step
tutorial for starting

00:13:01.851 --> 00:13:02.600
their own project?

00:13:02.600 --> 00:13:03.050
GABE WEISS: Totally.

00:13:03.050 --> 00:13:04.760
So if you go to Google
Cloud's website,

00:13:04.760 --> 00:13:07.505
cloud.google.com, click
on the documentation

00:13:07.505 --> 00:13:09.380
and there's a link to
everything right there.

00:13:15.165 --> 00:13:16.290
MARTIN KRONBERG: All right.

00:13:16.290 --> 00:13:18.150
Well, let's take a
look at some code

00:13:18.150 --> 00:13:21.434
and figure out how all
this stuff actually works.

00:13:21.434 --> 00:13:22.350
GABE WEISS: All right.

00:13:22.350 --> 00:13:24.180
So for the code,
there's the two pieces

00:13:24.180 --> 00:13:26.730
that I talked about before,
the JWT token creation

00:13:26.730 --> 00:13:29.550
and the MQTT client
initialization.

00:13:29.550 --> 00:13:31.890
So for the JWT token,
I'm highlighting here.

00:13:31.890 --> 00:13:35.160
This is really all of the
code it is-- very tight.

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:37.194
So for JWTs, there's
three properties

00:13:37.194 --> 00:13:38.610
that we're sending
for our tokens.

00:13:38.610 --> 00:13:41.680
There's an initialization
time, an expiration time,

00:13:41.680 --> 00:13:43.520
and then it's called
an audience key.

00:13:43.520 --> 00:13:47.490
And the audience key is
how the receiver knows

00:13:47.490 --> 00:13:48.750
what's being authenticated.

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:51.810
In our case, it's the
ID for the project

00:13:51.810 --> 00:13:54.920
that we created in the
Google Cloud Platform.

00:13:54.920 --> 00:13:57.000
I shows Intel webinar
as our project name.

00:13:57.000 --> 00:13:58.266
MARTIN KRONBERG: Right.

00:13:58.266 --> 00:13:59.640
GABE WEISS: Then
we can see here,

00:13:59.640 --> 00:14:01.800
we're grabbing the
private key, which

00:14:01.800 --> 00:14:03.340
lives on the device itself.

00:14:03.340 --> 00:14:05.370
That's what was flashed
onto the device.

00:14:05.370 --> 00:14:08.370
And that gets
encrypted with the JWT.

00:14:08.370 --> 00:14:11.710
And then we encode it and
send it with, in our case,

00:14:11.710 --> 00:14:15.150
we're using the RSA algorithm.

00:14:15.150 --> 00:14:16.290
So that's it for JWT token.

00:14:16.290 --> 00:14:19.440
The next piece is this
MQTT client initialization,

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:21.660
and that's this hunk
down here below.

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:26.490
And all it is, it's initialising
the MQTT client with an ID,

00:14:26.490 --> 00:14:29.850
and that ID is this
super-long URL, which

00:14:29.850 --> 00:14:34.740
is the ID for this individual
device in Google Cloud Platform

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:36.220
that we registered.

00:14:36.220 --> 00:14:40.230
And then an MQTT
topic, which is how

00:14:40.230 --> 00:14:42.270
the endpoint in
Google Cloud knows

00:14:42.270 --> 00:14:45.610
where to send your
individual, what

00:14:45.610 --> 00:14:49.530
bucket in Pub/Sub that its going
to end up identifying with.

00:14:49.530 --> 00:14:51.780
So the device is associated
with the registry.

00:14:51.780 --> 00:14:54.720
The registry is associated
with the Pub/Sub topic.

00:14:54.720 --> 00:14:58.740
And that's how it knows
where to put that message.

00:14:58.740 --> 00:15:01.230
From there, we established
our username/password.

00:15:01.230 --> 00:15:03.330
And for our password,
it is that JWT

00:15:03.330 --> 00:15:05.430
token that we created above.

00:15:05.430 --> 00:15:07.650
And a couple of
callbacks, so we can just

00:15:07.650 --> 00:15:11.890
track whats going on with the
client as it's doing its thing.

00:15:11.890 --> 00:15:13.530
And then we set the TLS.

00:15:13.530 --> 00:15:17.640
So the TLS is so that
the MQTT communication

00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:19.990
is authenticated on both sides.

00:15:19.990 --> 00:15:22.230
So the device is registered
with our SSL key.

00:15:22.230 --> 00:15:26.130
That's how the Google side knows
the device is talking to it.

00:15:26.130 --> 00:15:27.600
On the flip side,
the device needs

00:15:27.600 --> 00:15:30.000
to be assured that it's
actually talking to Google.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:34.080
For that we've retrieved a
root certificate from Google,

00:15:34.080 --> 00:15:35.910
and that's how then
the device knows

00:15:35.910 --> 00:15:39.000
it's talking to the cloud
service appropriately.

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:42.270
From there, this is
the MQTT endpoint

00:15:42.270 --> 00:15:46.050
that Google has created,
MQTT.googleAPIs.com,

00:15:46.050 --> 00:15:48.920
and that's how it knows where
to send the messages to.

00:15:48.920 --> 00:15:50.850
And that's it for the
cloud communication.

00:15:50.850 --> 00:15:51.210
MARTIN KRONBERG: Right.

00:15:51.210 --> 00:15:53.550
And so then, once we establish
that cloud communication

00:15:53.550 --> 00:15:58.770
to GCP, we're just going to
be creating a JSON object that

00:15:58.770 --> 00:16:02.790
has, as you guys see, a
timestamp, the temperature,

00:16:02.790 --> 00:16:06.270
the sound, and the light
values from our sensors.

00:16:06.270 --> 00:16:08.520
And we're getting the
temperature, the sound,

00:16:08.520 --> 00:16:12.000
and the light values
using those UPM libraries

00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:14.050
that I mentioned earlier.

00:16:14.050 --> 00:16:16.620
We're publishing this
to our MQTT topic,

00:16:16.620 --> 00:16:19.570
and we're doing
this once a second--

00:16:19.570 --> 00:16:23.850
basically, continuously until
we shut this script down.

00:16:23.850 --> 00:16:25.350
And that's all that
you really need

00:16:25.350 --> 00:16:26.910
in order to create
the connection

00:16:26.910 --> 00:16:28.784
and then start
sending data to GCP.

00:16:28.784 --> 00:16:30.450
GABE WEISS: So that's
it for the basics.

00:16:30.450 --> 00:16:31.890
Thanks everyone for
joining us today

00:16:31.890 --> 00:16:33.780
and learning about Intel
devices and starting

00:16:33.780 --> 00:16:36.750
to connect to Google's Cloud
Platform with those devices.

00:16:36.750 --> 00:16:38.250
Tune in to our next
webinar to learn

00:16:38.250 --> 00:16:40.290
about how the
messages from Pub/Sub

00:16:40.290 --> 00:16:42.000
can be fed into
Google's data pipeline

00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:43.155
to be analyzed and queried.

00:16:43.155 --> 00:16:44.530
MARTIN KRONBERG:
Thanks everyone,

00:16:44.530 --> 00:16:46.214
and we'll see you next time.

