00:19:07.661 --> 00:19:10.490
DIPAN SHETH: Yep.

00:19:10.490 --> 00:19:15.360
You mentioned that IoT Core
supports both MQTT and HTTPS

00:19:15.360 --> 00:19:16.460
in the video.

00:19:16.460 --> 00:19:18.320
Which one should they use?

00:19:18.320 --> 00:19:20.540
GABE WEISS: So it really
depends on your use case.

00:19:20.540 --> 00:19:25.880
Broadly speaking, my gut take
on IoT in general, use MQTT.

00:19:25.880 --> 00:19:29.225
HTTP sends a lot of extra stuff
along with the transmission--

00:19:29.225 --> 00:19:30.620
all your headers
and all of that.

00:19:30.620 --> 00:19:33.200
So since you're paying
for the amount of data

00:19:33.200 --> 00:19:35.390
that you transmit,
HTTP in the long term

00:19:35.390 --> 00:19:36.830
is going to cost you more.

00:19:36.830 --> 00:19:38.690
And also, because you're
sending more data,

00:19:38.690 --> 00:19:40.172
it's battery consumption.

00:19:40.172 --> 00:19:41.630
The biggest killer
of these devices

00:19:41.630 --> 00:19:43.170
is going to be network traffic.

00:19:43.170 --> 00:19:45.137
So the more traffic
you send, the quicker

00:19:45.137 --> 00:19:47.220
it's going to drain your
battery on these devices.

00:19:47.220 --> 00:19:50.270
So MQTT is becoming, basically,
the de facto standard

00:19:50.270 --> 00:19:53.370
in the industry, so
that's what we suggest.

00:19:53.370 --> 00:19:54.290
DIPAN SHETH: Great.

00:19:54.290 --> 00:19:54.790
Great.

00:19:54.790 --> 00:19:58.350
So the other one is, what
kind of quality of service

00:19:58.350 --> 00:20:00.630
do you support for
the MQTT protocol?

00:20:00.630 --> 00:20:01.380
GABE WEISS: Right.

00:20:01.380 --> 00:20:03.187
So there's three
different levels

00:20:03.187 --> 00:20:05.770
of service for those that don't
know about quality of service.

00:20:05.770 --> 00:20:07.949
There's no guarantee that
the message got sent,

00:20:07.949 --> 00:20:09.990
so you just kind of fire
it off into the internet

00:20:09.990 --> 00:20:12.060
and pray that it gets
to where it needs to go.

00:20:12.060 --> 00:20:15.120
There's guaranteed
at least once,

00:20:15.120 --> 00:20:17.160
and there's guaranteed
once and only once.

00:20:17.160 --> 00:20:20.160
So the levels that we support
are that fire and forget,

00:20:20.160 --> 00:20:22.830
level 0, and level 1,
which is at least once.

00:20:22.830 --> 00:20:25.860
We don't support level 2, which
is the once and only once.

00:20:25.860 --> 00:20:28.980
The performance hit
on it isn't scalable,

00:20:28.980 --> 00:20:31.987
and it won't work
well with iOS devices.

